  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Show that each function is decreasing on each interval of its domain.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=-2x+6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=-2<0$ for all real $x$.",
          "worked": "$$y'=-2<0$$\nSo the function is decreasing for all real $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 11
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=-x^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=-3x^2<0$ for $x\\ne 0$, and $y'=0$ at $x=0$. The function is decreasing throughout its domain.",
          "worked": "$$y'=-3x^2$$\nThe derivative is negative on both sides of $0$ and zero at $0$. The function decreases on each side. Its values are positive for $x<0$, zero at $0$ and negative for $x>0$, so it decreases across $0$ as well.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=\\frac{4x}{x-2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=-\\frac{8}{(x-2)^2}<0$ for $x\\ne 2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{4(x-2)-4x}{(x-2)^2}\\\\y'&=-\\frac{8}{(x-2)^2}<0\\end{align*}$$\nThe denominator is positive for $x\\ne 2$. The function decreases on $(-\\infty,2)$ and $(2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      }
    ]
  },
